From: (local patch, not an upstream commit) QM1 Debian Maintainers <qm1-debian@users.noreply.github.com>
Date: Sat, 7 Nov 2026 12:00:00 +0800
Subject: [PATCH] wifi: rtw88: sdio: reject oversized RX packets

Carried from the upstream rtw-next commit

  83ca360e85280ecaf0ded1b84d184a2839fea20f
  "wifi: rtw88: sdio: reject oversized RX packets"
  (Luka Gejak, 2026-10-09)

which is *not* part of the upstream snapshot this module is built from
(05441d697f7f9a927264f4058cbbc6a03dfcac9d, a torvalds/linux master
snapshot of 2026-11-03): master only merged rtw-next up to the
"rtw-next-2026-10-08" tag, so every 8723B fix from 2026-10-09 onwards
is missing from the tree we ship.

Why we carry it:

  rtw_sdio_rxfifo_recv() takes the last-entry path when the RX
  descriptor claims more data than rx_len.  The skb is then handed to
  rtw_sdio_rx_skb(), which calls skb_put() with pkt_len without ever
  checking it against the space that is left.  A bad descriptor can
  therefore overflow the skb, which is kernel memory corruption.

  The QM1's RTL8723BS is an SDIO device and its only network link, so
  an RX path that can corrupt memory is not an acceptable risk.  The
  fix is eight lines and self-contained.

---
 sdio.c | 8 ++++++++
 1 file changed, 8 insertions(+)
diff --git a/sdio.c b/sdio.c
--- a/sdio.c
+++ b/sdio.c
@@ -1389,6 +1389,14 @@
 		curr_pkt_len = ALIGN(pkt_offset + pkt_stat.pkt_len,
 				     RTW_SDIO_DATA_PTR_ALIGN);
 
+		if (unlikely(pkt_offset + pkt_stat.pkt_len > rx_len)) {
+			rtw_dbg(rtwdev, RTW_DBG_SDIO,
+				"oversized RX packet: %u, %u bytes left\n",
+				pkt_offset + pkt_stat.pkt_len, rx_len);
+			dev_kfree_skb_any(skb);
+			return;
+		}
+
 		if ((curr_pkt_len + pkt_desc_sz) >= rx_len) {
 			/* Use the original skb (with it's adjusted offset)
 			 * when processing the last (or even the only) entry to
